// D1543 — an ANONYMOUS struct literal whose STRUCT-valued field is NOT itself a literal,
// delivered everywhere. `collectAnonShapes` mints only when EVERY field has a code, and the
// name ladder codes a field by its RENDERED type — which for a struct value is the
// STRUCTURAL `{n: i32}` even where the value carries a declared alias. The field coded -1 and
// the OUTER literal got no row: `ref valtype with no interned shape`.
//
// THE FIELD VALUE MUST STAY A CALL. Writing the nested literal inline (`{ item: { n: 3 } }`)
// is the spelling that always worked, so an inline version of this template greens every cell
// for the wrong reason — the same trap D1443's own header names one row over.
//
// The PROOF reads THROUGH the field (`v.item.n`), so a row whose target came from the wrong
// shape traps or prints the wrong number rather than passing.
//
// Format: scripts/capability-probes/matrix.py, whose docstring is the spec.
// @@PRELUDE@@
function mkRecA(): { n: i32 } { return { n: 3 } }
function mkRecB(): { n: i32 } { return { n: 4 } }
// @@VALUE@@
{ item: mkRecA() }
// @@TYPE@@
{ item: {n: i32} }
// @@PROOF@@
print(v.item.n)
// @@WANT@@
3
// @@VALUE2@@
{ item: mkRecB() }
// @@TYPE2@@
{ item: {n: i32} }
// @@PROOF2@@
print(v.item.n + 1)
// @@WANT2@@
5
// @@SKIP@@
is_in_if: the value is a struct, not a union — there is no arm to test it against
is_in_while: same
is_in_and: same
is_in_not: same
else_if: same
early_return_guard: same
